[Chapter "Queen's Gambit"]
[Group "1...d5: the Queen's Gambit"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit"]
[ECO "D06"]
[Diagram "2.c4"]
[Course "queens-gambit-declined queens-gambit-accepted slav tarrasch"]
[Page "qg"]

{White offers the c-pawn to lure Black's d-pawn away from the center. Black declines it with 2...e6 or 2...c6, or accepts it with 2...dxc4.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 {This is the Queen's Gambit.} *

[Chapter "London System"]
[Course "london"]
[Group "1...d5: the London System"]
[Opening "Queen's Pawn Game: Accelerated London System"]
[ECO "D00"]
[Diagram "4...c5"]

{White's setup: Bf4, e3, Nf3, c3 and Nbd2. The c3–d4–e3 pawn triangle is hard to break.

White: Play the same setup almost every game: d4, Bf4, e3, Nf3, c3, Nbd2, Bd3 and castling. The c3–d4–e3 pawn triangle is hard to break. Later, Ne5 and a kingside attack are typical.

Black: Keep the setup you play against the Queen's Gambit: ...Nf6, ...e6, ...c5 and ...Bd6 to offer a trade of the London bishop, then ...Qc7, ...Nbd7 and the break ...e5. After 1...Nf6, the King's Indian setup works too.}
1. d4 d5 2. Bf4 {This is the London System. The bishop comes out before e3 can shut it in.} Nf6
3. e3 {Builds the pawn triangle.} e6
4. Nf3 c5 {The most active answer: Black hits d4 at once.} *

[Chapter "Jobava London System"]
[Map "Jobava London"]
[Course "jobava-london"]
[Group "1...d5: the London System"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]
[Diagram "4...Na6"]

{A sharper London: the knight goes to c3 instead of the c-pawn, and the bishop to f4. The knight can jump to b5, and White's h-pawn often joins the attack.

White: Play d4, Nc3 and Bf4 against both 1...d5 and 1...Nf6, then e3. Against ...e6, jump to b5 to hit c7. Trade off Black's bishop with Bd3 if it comes to f5, and push h4 against ...Bg6 or ...g6.

Black: Guard d5 with ...e6, pin the c3 knight with ...Bb4, and hit d4 with ...c5. If White plays a3, take on c3 and hit the doubled pawn with ...Qa5. Against Nb5, check on b4 first.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 {The knight comes out in front of the c-pawn.} Nf6
3. Bf4 {This is the Jobava London. Lichess calls it the Rapport–Jobava System.} e6
4. Nb5 {[%cal Gb5c7] The knight and the bishop both hit c7.} Na6 {The most common answer: the knight guards c7.} *

[Chapter "King's Indian Defense"]
[Map "King's Indian"]
[MapReply "Indian Defenses"]
[Course "kings-indian"]
[Group "1...Nf6: the Indian Defenses"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense"]
[ECO "E61"]
[Diagram "4...d6"]

{White: Build a big center with c4, d4 and e4, develop calmly with Nf3 and Be2, and castle. When the center closes with d5, attack on the queenside with c5, b4 and the c-file.

Black: Let White have the center, fianchetto your bishop and castle. Then strike at the center with ...e5 or ...c5. Once the center is closed, attack the king with ...f5, ...f4 and ...g5: the game becomes a race.}
1. d4 Nf6 {Stops e4 and keeps Black's options open: the start of the Indian defenses.}
2. c4 g6 {Prepares to fianchetto the bishop.}
3. Nc3 Bg7 {This is the King's Indian Defense. The bishop will press on the long diagonal.}
4. e4 {White takes the whole center.} d6 {Stops e5 and prepares ...e5 or ...c5.} *

[Chapter "Nimzo-Indian Defense"]
[Map "Nimzo-Indian"]
[MapReply "Indian Defenses"]
[Course "nimzo-indian"]
[Group "1...Nf6: the Indian Defenses"]
[Opening "Nimzo-Indian Defense"]
[ECO "E20"]
[Diagram "4...O-O"]

{White: 4.Qc2 lets you take back on c3 with the queen, so you avoid doubled pawns; then a3 wins the bishop pair and e4 takes the center. Or play 4.e3 and develop quickly with Bd3, Nf3 and castling.

Black: Pin the c3 knight so White can't play e4. Give up the bishop for the knight when it doubles White's c-pawns or costs White time. Strike back with ...c5 or ...d5.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 {Prepares ...d5 or ...Bb4.}
3. Nc3 {Prepares e4.} Bb4 {This is the Nimzo-Indian Defense. The pinned knight can't support e4.}
4. Qc2 {The Classical Variation. If Black takes on c3, the queen takes back and White's pawns stay whole.} O-O {Black castles and keeps ...d5, ...c5 and ...Bxc3 in reserve.} *

[Chapter "Other Indian Defenses"]
[Map "Other Indian Defenses"]
[MapReply "Indian Defenses"]
[Group "1...Nf6: the Indian Defenses"]
[Opening "Indian Defense: Normal Variation"]
[ECO "A50"]
[Diagram "2.c4"]
[Page "indian"]

{Black develops the knight first and keeps the choice of setup open. The other choices are the Grünfeld, the Queen's Indian and the Benoni; the Indian Defenses page shows them beside the King's Indian and the Nimzo-Indian.}
1. d4 Nf6 {Black stops e4 and waits to choose a setup.} 2. c4 {The most common reply. White takes more of the center.} *

